# ~/.config/agentbox/networks — docker networks to attach this container to, one name per line.
#
# Each line is a single docker network name (e.g. `lab`). `#` starts a comment; a trailing
# comment is allowed. One name per line — a second token on a line is rejected, and names are
# [a-zA-Z0-9_.-]+ (a value starting with `-` is rejected so `docker network connect` can't read
# it as a flag).
#
# Why a separate file (not a `--network` flag): joining a docker network is done with
# `docker network connect`, an OUTER-daemon operation on an EXISTING container. ab can't do it
# from inside the Sysbox container, so it runs AFTER the container is up (in `ab start` /
# `ab rebuild`), not as a `docker run` flag at create time.
#
# The point: reach other containers on a shared docker network by NAME. A container already on
# `lab` (e.g. a database the host runs via `docker compose up`) is then reachable from inside
# agentbox as `lab`-network DNS — `host: <container-name>`. No host port needs to be published,
# and the `ports` / `host.docker.internal` mechanism isn't involved.
#
# Apply: networks attach after start, NOT at creation, so a plain `ab stop && ab start` picks up
# changes (no rebuild). Already-attached networks are a no-op (idempotent across `ab start`). A
# network that doesn't exist, or a line that fails to parse, is reported and skipped — it won't
# stop the container.
#
# Note: this is skipped on the already-running early-return path of `ab start`, so adding a
# network to a RUNNING container needs `ab stop && ab start`.
lab
